CAT = "aatl2ControlBank"; SEG = "CTL"
Q = [
("The receivables ledger control account opens at £24,600 debit. Credit sales £61,200, receipts £58,350, sales returns £1,940, discounts allowed £410, and an irrecoverable debt of £600 is written off. The closing balance is:",
 "£24,500", ["£25,100", "£26,440", "£24,910"],
 "£24,600 + £61,200 - £58,350 - £1,940 - £410 - £600 = £24,500. Forgetting the write-off gives £25,100, and forgetting the returns £26,440."),
("Which item is credited to the payables ledger control account?",
 "Credit purchases from the daybook", ["Payments made to suppliers", "Purchases returns", "Discounts received"],
 "Credit purchases increase what the business owes, so they are credited. Payments, returns and discounts received all reduce the liability and are debited."),
("The VAT control account shows output VAT of £9,840 and input VAT of £6,215. The business:",
 "Owes HMRC £3,625", ["Is owed £3,625 by HMRC", "Owes HMRC £9,840", "Owes HMRC £16,055"],
 "Output VAT on sales is owed to HMRC, less input VAT reclaimed on purchases: £9,840 - £6,215 = £3,625 payable."),
("Which entry in the VAT control account increases the amount owed to HMRC?",
 "VAT on credit sales", ["VAT on credit purchases", "VAT on sales returns", "VAT on discounts allowed"],
 "VAT charged on sales is a credit in the VAT control account and adds to the liability. Purchases, sales returns and discounts allowed all reduce it."),
("The list of receivables ledger balances totals £18,320 and the control account £18,770. An invoice of £450 was entered in the sales daybook but never posted to the customer's account. Once it is posted, the list totals:",
 "£18,770", ["£17,870", "£18,320", "£19,220"],
 "Posting the missing invoice adds £450 to the list: £18,320 + £450 = £18,770, which agrees with the control account."),
("Why is the receivables ledger control account reconciled with the receivables ledger?",
 "To find errors in either record", ["To work out the VAT owed", "To calculate the year's net profit", "To agree the bank balance"],
 "Both records come from the same daybooks, so they should agree. A difference points to a posting error in one of them."),
("One customer's account in the receivables ledger has a credit balance. The most likely reason is that the customer:",
 "Overpaid or was given a credit", ["Exceeded their credit limit", "Bought goods for cash", "Has not paid an invoice"],
 "A credit balance means the business owes the customer, usually after an overpayment or a credit note issued after payment. An unpaid invoice gives a debit balance."),
("A discount received of £60 was entered in the discounts received daybook but never posted to the supplier's account. Compared with the control account, the list of payables ledger balances is:",
 "£60 higher", ["£60 lower", "£120 higher", "The same"],
 "The discount should have reduced the supplier's balance. It reached the control account but not the supplier's account, so the list is £60 higher."),
("Which payment method transfers money to the payee on the same working day and is often used to complete a property purchase?",
 "CHAPS", ["BACS", "Standing order", "Cheque"],
 "CHAPS is a same-day high-value payment system. BACS payments take about three working days to arrive."),
("A business tells its bank to pay the same amount to its landlord on the 1st of each month. This is a:",
 "Standing order", ["Direct debit", "Bank draft", "CHAPS payment"],
 "A standing order is set up by the payer for a fixed amount on fixed dates. A direct debit is collected by the payee and can vary."),
("Which payment lets a supplier collect varying amounts from the payer's account on agreed dates?",
 "Direct debit", ["Standing order", "Faster Payment", "Bank draft"],
 "With a direct debit the payer authorises the payee to collect the amount due, which can change. Utility bills are often paid this way."),
("Paying a supplier by cheque reduces the business's bank balance:",
 "When the cheque is presented", ["On the day the cheque is written", "When the invoice arrives", "On the day it is posted"],
 "Funds leave the account when the supplier pays the cheque in and it clears. Until then it is an unpresented cheque on the bank reconciliation."),
("A cheque the business has sent to a supplier, which has not yet appeared on the bank statement, is:",
 "An unpresented cheque", ["An outstanding lodgement", "A bank error", "A dishonoured cheque"],
 "An unpresented cheque is in the cash book but not yet on the statement. An outstanding lodgement is money paid in that the bank has not yet recorded."),
("Which item on the bank statement must be entered in the cash book when it is updated?",
 "Bank charges", ["Unpresented cheques", "Outstanding lodgements", "Cheques written today"],
 "Bank charges appear first on the statement, so the cash book is updated for them. Unpresented cheques and outstanding lodgements are timing differences already in the cash book."),
("The bank statement shows £6,480 in funds. Unpresented cheques total £1,915 and outstanding lodgements £740. The cash book balance should be:",
 "£5,305", ["£7,655", "£3,825", "£8,395"],
 "£6,480 - £1,915 + £740 = £5,305 in funds. Unpresented cheques will reduce the bank balance and outstanding lodgements will increase it."),
("The cash book shows £3,120 in funds. The bank statement shows a direct debit of £185 and bank interest received of £12, not yet in the cash book. The updated cash book balance is:",
 "£2,947", ["£2,923", "£3,293", "£3,317"],
 "£3,120 - £185 + £12 = £2,947. Deducting the interest instead of adding it gives £2,923."),
("An outstanding lodgement is:",
 "Money banked but not yet on the statement", ["A cheque the payee has not yet banked", "A receipt the bank recorded twice", "A standing order left out of the cash book"],
 "An outstanding lodgement is in the cash book but has not yet reached the bank statement, so it is added to the statement balance on the reconciliation."),
("A customer pays by Faster Payments. Its effect on the bank balance is that the money is:",
 "Available almost at once", ["Available after three working days", "Available once a cheque clears", "Not counted until month end"],
 "Faster Payments usually arrive within minutes and at most a few hours. BACS takes about three working days."),
("On a bank reconciliation that starts from the bank statement balance, outstanding lodgements are:",
 "Added to the statement balance", ["Deducted from the statement balance", "Added to the cash book balance", "Entered as bank charges"],
 "The bank will record the lodgements soon, so they are added to the statement balance to arrive at the cash book balance."),
("The receipts side of the cash book was overcast by £90 (added up £90 too high). The correction is to:",
 "Reduce the cash book balance by £90", ["Increase the cash book balance by £90", "Show £90 on the reconciliation", "Ask the bank to correct its records"],
 "The error is in the business's own records, so the cash book is corrected. Only bank errors and timing differences go on the reconciliation."),
("The payables ledger control account has a credit balance of £14,280. A purchases returns credit note of £320 had been credited to it instead of debited. The corrected balance is:",
 "£13,640", ["£13,960", "£14,600", "£14,920"],
 "Removing the wrong credit (£320) and making the right debit (£320) reduces the balance by £640: £14,280 - £640 = £13,640."),
("The VAT control account has a debit balance at the end of a VAT period. This means:",
 "HMRC owes the business a refund", ["The business owes VAT to HMRC now", "Output VAT exceeded input VAT", "The VAT return was filed late"],
 "A debit balance means input VAT reclaimable is more than output VAT due, so HMRC repays the difference."),
("When VAT on an irrecoverable debt can be reclaimed, the VAT control account is:",
 "Debited, reducing VAT owed", ["Credited, increasing VAT owed", "Left without any entry", "Debited with the gross debt"],
 "The VAT element of the debt is debited to VAT control, reducing what is owed to HMRC. The net is debited to irrecoverable debts and the gross credited to receivables."),
("A supplier's account in the payables ledger has a debit balance of £75. The most likely reason is that the business:",
 "Overpaid that supplier", ["Has an unpaid invoice", "Bought on credit from them", "Received their invoice twice"],
 "Supplier accounts normally have credit balances. A debit balance means the supplier owes the business, usually after an overpayment or a late credit note."),
]
